### What is the Daily Temperatures problem?

Given an array of temperatures, return an array where each position holds the number of days until a strictly warmer day. If no warmer day exists, the answer is 0. This is the Next Greater Element pattern, but the answer is the distance rather than the value.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Deque;

public class DailyTemperatures {
    public static int[] dailyTemperatures(int[] temps) {
        int[] answer = new int[temps.length];
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(); // indices

        for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && temps[i] > temps[stack.peek()]) {
                int prev = stack.pop();
                answer[prev] = i - prev;
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73};
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(dailyTemperatures(temps)));
    }
}
```

```text
[1, 1, 4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0]
```

### Why store indices instead of values?

The answer is the gap in days, which is the difference between two indices. If you stored values, you would know a warmer day exists but not how far away it is. Storing indices lets you compute `i - prev` directly.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class WhyIndices {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {71, 69, 72};
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && temps[i] > temps[stack.peek()]) {
                int prev = stack.pop();
                int gap = i - prev;
                System.out.println("Day " + prev + " (" + temps[prev] + ") resolved by day " + i + " (" + temps[i] + "), gap=" + gap);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        System.out.println("Unresolved indices: " + stack);
    }
}
```

```text
Day 1 (69) resolved by day 2 (72), gap=1
Day 0 (71) resolved by day 2 (72), gap=2
Unresolved indices: [2]
```

### How does the monotonic decreasing stack work?

The stack holds indices in decreasing temperature order from bottom to top. When a new temperature is higher than the top, the top can be resolved. You keep popping until the stack top is warmer or equal, then push the current index. This maintains the decreasing invariant.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MonotonicDecreasingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73};
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && temps[i] > temps[stack.peek()]) {
                stack.pop();
            }
            stack.push(i);
            System.out.println("After index " + i + " (" + temps[i] + "): stack indices = " + stack);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
After index 0 (73): stack indices = [0]
After index 1 (74): stack indices = [1]
After index 2 (75): stack indices = [2]
After index 3 (71): stack indices = [3, 2]
After index 4 (69): stack indices = [4, 3, 2]
After index 5 (72): stack indices = [5, 2]
After index 6 (76): stack indices = [6]
After index 7 (73): stack indices = [7, 6]
```

### Why use strictly-greater comparison?

The problem asks for a strictly warmer day. Using `>=` would let equal temperatures resolve each other, which is wrong. If two consecutive days are both 72 degrees, neither is warmer than the other, and using `>=` would falsely report a wait of 1 day.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StrictlyGreater {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {72, 72, 73};

        // With > (correct): day 0 waits for day 2 (73), gap=2
        // With >= (wrong):  day 0 resolved by day 1 (72), gap=1
        System.out.println("temps[1]=72 is NOT warmer than temps[0]=72");
        System.out.println("Use > not >= to ensure strictly warmer");
        System.out.println("Correct answer for day 0: 2 (waits for day 2)");
    }
}
```

```text
temps[1]=72 is NOT warmer than temps[0]=72
Use > not >= to ensure strictly warmer
Correct answer for day 0: 2 (waits for day 2)
```

### What about the unresolved days?

Indices remaining on the stack after the scan have no warmer day ahead. Since the answer array is initialized to 0 by default in Java, these entries are already correct. No separate cleanup loop is needed.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class UnresolvedDays {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] answer = new int[5];
        System.out.println("Default values: " + Arrays.toString(answer));
        // All zeros: perfect for unresolved days
        System.out.println("Unresolved days keep their default 0");
    }
}
```

```text
Default values: [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
Unresolved days keep their default 0
```

### What is the time complexity despite the inner loop?

The inner while loop might look like it makes the algorithm O(n^2), but each index is pushed once and popped at most once. The total number of push and pop operations across all iterations is at most 2n, so the overall time is O(n). This is a standard amortized argument for monotonic stack algorithms.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ComplexityProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // For n elements:
        // Total pushes:  exactly n (one per element)
        // Total pops:    at most n (each element popped at most once)
        // Total work:    at most 2n = O(n)
        System.out.println("Each index: pushed once, popped at most once");
        System.out.println("Total operations: at most 2n");
        System.out.println("Time complexity: O(n)");
    }
}
```

```text
Each index: pushed once, popped at most once
Total operations: at most 2n
Time complexity: O(n)
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, I used this to compute a days-to-recovery metric for a daily market sentiment index. For each day, the dashboard shows how many days passed before sentiment rose above that day's level. One linear pass produces the full array over a long trading history.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Deque;

public class SentimentRecovery {
    public static int[] daysToRecovery(double[] scores) {
        int[] result = new int[scores.length];
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && scores[i] > scores[stack.peek()]) {
                int prev = stack.pop();
                result[prev] = i - prev;
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[] sentiment = {7.2, 6.8, 6.5, 7.0, 7.5, 7.1};
        System.out.println("Sentiment scores: " + Arrays.toString(sentiment));
        System.out.println("Days to recovery: " + Arrays.toString(daysToRecovery(sentiment)));
    }
}
```

```text
Sentiment scores: [7.2, 6.8, 6.5, 7.0, 7.5, 7.1]
Days to recovery: [4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This is the Next Greater Element pattern. The same decreasing stack technique finds the next greater element, the next greater element in a circular array (by iterating twice), and the stock span problem (days since the last higher price). Recognizing the monotonic stack pattern across these variants is what interviewers look for at a senior level.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MonotonicStackVariants {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pattern: Monotonic Decreasing Stack");
        System.out.println("Variant 1: Next Greater Element -> push values, return value");
        System.out.println("Variant 2: Daily Temperatures -> push indices, return distance");
        System.out.println("Variant 3: Circular NGE -> iterate array twice (i % n)");
        System.out.println("Variant 4: Stock Span -> days since last higher price");
    }
}
```

```text
Pattern: Monotonic Decreasing Stack
Variant 1: Next Greater Element -> push values, return value
Variant 2: Daily Temperatures -> push indices, return distance
Variant 3: Circular NGE -> iterate array twice (i % n)
Variant 4: Stock Span -> days since last higher price
```
